{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"functions","title":"Functions: Declarations, Expressions & Arrows","group":"Functions & Scope","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Functions are first-class objects: create them three ways, pass them around, return them — and pick arrows or regular functions for the right reasons.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Three ways to define a function</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>// 1. Declaration — hoisted completely\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\n// 2. Expression — a value assigned to a variable (optionally named)\nconst multiply = function mul(a, b) {\n  return a * b;\n};\n// 3. Arrow function (ES2015) — concise, lexical `this`\nconst square = (x) =&gt; x * x;\nconst makePoint = (x, y) =&gt; ({ x, y });   // wrap object literals in ( )\n\nconsole.log(add(2, 3), multiply(2, 3), square(4), makePoint(1, 2));\nconsole.log(multiply.name, square.name, add.length);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 6 16 { x: 1, y: 2 }\nmul square 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-up\"></i>Declarations are hoisted, expressions are not</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(early());          // works: whole declaration is hoisted\nfunction early() { return \"declared\"; }\n\ntry {\n  late();\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.name + \": \" + e.message);\n}\nconst late = () =&gt; \"expression\";</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>declared\nReferenceError: Cannot access 'late' before initialization</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Arrow vs regular functions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Regular <code>function</code></th><th>Arrow <code>=&gt;</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>this</code></td><td>Set by <em>how it is called</em></td><td>Inherited from the enclosing scope (lexical)</td></tr><tr><td><code>arguments</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No (use rest <code>...args</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Can be used with <code>new</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No — TypeError</td></tr><tr><td><code>prototype</code> property</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Hoisting</td><td>Declarations are hoisted</td><td>Like any <code>const</code> — TDZ</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Methods, constructors, generators</td><td>Callbacks, short transforms, keeping outer <code>this</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const Arrow = () =&gt; {};\ntry {\n  new Arrow();\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}\nconsole.log(Arrow.prototype, typeof function () {}.prototype);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Arrow is not a constructor\nundefined object</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Functions are objects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function greet(name) { return `Hi ${name}`; }\ngreet.calls = 0;                       // attach properties like any object\nconst fns = [greet, (s) =&gt; s.toUpperCase()];\nconsole.log(fns.map((f) =&gt; f(\"x\")));\nconsole.log(typeof greet, greet instanceof Object, greet);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 'Hi x', 'X' ]\nfunction true [Function: greet] { calls: 0 }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back\"></i>Return values</h3>\n<ul><li>A function without <code>return</code> (or with a bare <code>return;</code>) returns <code>undefined</code>.</li><li>Arrow with a <em>concise body</em> (<code>x =&gt; x * 2</code>) returns the expression; with braces you need <code>return</code>.</li><li>Automatic semicolon insertion: <code>return</code> followed by a newline returns <code>undefined</code> — keep the value on the same line.</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function broken() {\n  return\n    { ok: true };\n}\nconst withBraces = (x) =&gt; { x * 2 };\nconsole.log(broken(), withBraces(2));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>undefined undefined</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between a function declaration and a function expression?</summary><div><p>A declaration (<code>function f() {}</code>) is a statement that is hoisted with its body, so it can be called before it appears. An expression creates the function as a value (assigned to a variable, passed as an argument); it only exists once that line runs.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When should you NOT use an arrow function?</summary><div><p>As an object method that needs <code>this</code> to be the object, as a constructor, as a prototype method, as an event handler that needs <code>this</code> to be the element, or when you need <code>arguments</code> or a generator.</p></div></details></section>"}